1. "I am invoking my right to remain silent."
2. "I want a lawyer."
3. "Am I being detained or am I free to go?"
4. "I do not consent to a search."
5. (Your name/ID only if your state requires it)

Say them clearly and stop talking. Police may lie during interrogations — it is legal. Anything you say can be used against you. Your silence after invoking cannot be used against you at trial.',25,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (2,'Miranda Rights: What They Actually Mean','Arrest Rights','Miranda rights only apply when BOTH are true:
1. You are in custody (not free to leave)
2. Police are interrogating you

The four rights:
• Right to remain silent
• Anything you say can be used against you
• Right to an attorney
• Right to a free attorney if you cannot afford one

To invoke: Say "I am invoking my right to remain silent and I want a lawyer." After that, all questioning must stop. Do not just go quiet — you must say the words.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (3,'What Police Can and Cannot Do During a Stop','Arrest Rights','POLICE CAN:
• Ask your name (required in 24 stop-and-identify states)
• Pat down outer clothing if they suspect a weapon
• Search your car with probable cause
• Search incident to arrest

POLICE CANNOT:
• Search your phone without a warrant (Riley v. California 2014)
• Enter your home without a warrant, consent, or emergency
• Arrest you for refusing to answer questions alone
• Use excessive force

If rights are violated: Do NOT resist. Say "I do not consent to this search." Document everything and tell your attorney immediately.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (4,'Stop and Identify Laws by State','Arrest Rights','24 states require you to provide your name when lawfully stopped:

MUST GIVE NAME: AL, AR, AZ, CO, DE, FL, GA, IL, IN, KS, LA, MO, MT, NE, NV, NH, NM, NY, ND, OH, TX, UT, WI, WY

NO REQUIREMENT (includes CA, TN, WA, OR):
You are not required to give your name during a stop — but must show driver''s license if driving.

In stop-and-identify states, you only need your NAME — not answers to questions, not explanation of where you''re going.',15,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (5,'Your Rights at a DUI Checkpoint','DUI','AT A DUI CHECKPOINT:
✅ Must stop at a lawful checkpoint
✅ Must provide: license, registration, insurance
✅ May remain silent beyond basic ID
✅ May decline to answer "Have you been drinking?"

❌ Field Sobriety Tests: Voluntary — you can refuse. They rarely help and often hurt.
❌ Roadside breathalyzer (pre-arrest): Can refuse in most states
⚠️ Post-arrest breathalyzer: Refusal = automatic license suspension in all 50 states.

📅 CRITICAL: After DUI arrest you typically have only 7-10 days to request a DMV hearing. Miss this and you automatically lose your license.',25,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (6,'The 10-Day Rule After a DUI Arrest','DUI','The most-missed deadline after a DUI arrest:

After your license is taken at a DUI stop, you have a narrow window to request a DMV administrative hearing — separate from the criminal case.

STATE DEADLINES:
• California: 10 days
• Texas: 15 days
• Florida: 10 days
• New York: 7 days
• Tennessee: 10 days
• Illinois: 90 days

Miss this deadline = automatic license suspension, even if criminal charges are dropped.

Call a DUI attorney within 24 hours of arrest.',25,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (7,'Drug Charges: Possession vs. Intent to Distribute','Drug Offenses','The difference is enormous:

SIMPLE POSSESSION:
• Personal use amount
• Misdemeanor to low-level felony
• Probation often possible

POSSESSION WITH INTENT TO DISTRIBUTE:
• Based on: quantity, packaging, scales, large cash
• Felony in every state
• Mandatory minimums in many states

Key defenses:
• Unlawful search and seizure (4th Amendment — most common)
• Lack of knowledge
• Not yours (constructive possession)
• Lab testing challenges

NEVER consent to a search.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (8,'What Happens After You Are Arrested: Step by Step','Court Process','THE CRIMINAL COURT PROCESS:

1. ARREST
2. BOOKING — Fingerprints, photo, record check (1-4 hours)
3. ARRAIGNMENT — First court date within 24-72 hours. Bail set. Always plead NOT GUILTY.
4. PRELIMINARY HEARING — Judge checks probable cause (felonies)
5. DISCOVERY — Evidence exchanged
6. PRE-TRIAL MOTIONS — Suppress evidence, dismiss charges
7. PLEA NEGOTIATION — 90%+ of cases end here
8. TRIAL — If no plea deal
9. SENTENCING
10. APPEAL

Always plead NOT GUILTY at arraignment even if you plan to take a plea later.',25,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (9,'How Bail Works','Bail','TYPES OF RELEASE:
• Personal Recognizance (PR): Released on your word — no money required
• Cash Bail: Pay full amount to court — refunded at end of case
• Bail Bond: Pay bondsman 10% (non-refundable) — they guarantee the rest
• Property Bond: Real estate as collateral
• No Bail (Remanded): Judge denies bail

HOW A BAIL BONDSMAN WORKS:
1. Pay 10% to bondsman (you never get this back)
2. Bondsman posts full bail
3. Miss court = bondsman loses money and will find you
4. Even if found not guilty, bondsman keeps the 10%

FACTORS JUDGES CONSIDER: Charge severity, criminal history, community ties, flight risk, danger to community',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (10,'Can You Clear Your Record? Expungement Explained','Expungement','Expungement legally seals or erases a criminal record. After expungement, you can answer "no" on most job applications about criminal history.

WHO MAY QUALIFY:
• First-time offenders
• Non-violent offenses
• Sentence completed including probation
• Waiting period after discharge (1-10 years depending on state)
• No pending charges

CAN BE EXPUNGED: Arrests with no conviction, dismissed charges, misdemeanors, some felonies

CANNOT (usually): Sex offenses, violent felonies, federal convictions, some DUIs

AUTOMATIC SEALING: CA, MI, NY, PA, WA now automatically seal eligible records after waiting periods.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (11,'What Is a Public Defender?','Legal Rights','The 6th Amendment guarantees you an attorney if you face potential jail time and cannot afford one.

HOW TO GET ONE:
At arraignment, tell the judge: "I cannot afford an attorney and I am requesting a court-appointed attorney." Complete a financial questionnaire.

ELIGIBILITY: Generally 125% of federal poverty level — varies by jurisdiction.

PUBLIC DEFENDERS ARE REAL ATTORNEYS:
• Licensed by the state bar
• Often specialize exclusively in criminal defense
• Same authority as private attorneys
• Can file motions, negotiate pleas, go to trial

REALITY: Many are overloaded. Stay in contact, document everything, know your rights.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (12,'Domestic Violence Charges: Both Sides','Domestic Violence','DV LAW IS DIFFERENT:
• Police usually MUST arrest if they see signs of DV
• The victim cannot "drop the charges" — the STATE prosecutes
• A protective order is often issued at arrest
• Violating a protective order is a new crime

IF ACCUSED:
• Do not contact the alleged victim even if they want contact
• Comply fully with any protective order
• Get an attorney immediately
• Document your account while memory is fresh

IF VICTIM:
• You have the right to leave immediately
• You do not have to prosecute
• National DV Hotline: (800) 799-7233

MUTUAL COMBAT: Both parties can be arrested. The person who called police can still be charged.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (13,'Search and Seizure: Your 4th Amendment Rights','Legal Rights','POLICE NEED A WARRANT for most searches — home, car, phone.

EXCEPTIONS (no warrant needed):
• You consent (say: "I do not consent")
• Search incident to arrest
• Plain view (contraband visible)
• Exigent circumstances (emergency)
• Vehicle exception (probable cause)
• Border crossings

YOUR PHONE: Riley v. California (2014) — police CANNOT search your phone without a warrant. They can seize it but not look through it.

ILLEGAL SEARCH:
• Do not physically resist
• Clearly state "I do not consent"
• Document officer names, badge numbers, witnesses
• Tell attorney immediately — illegally obtained evidence can be suppressed',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (14,'Juvenile Rights in the Justice System','Juvenile','JUVENILE COURT IS DIFFERENT:
• Age cutoff usually 18 (some states 17)
• Hearings not public
• "Adjudicated delinquent" not "convicted"
• Focus on rehabilitation
• Records can often be sealed at 18

JUVENILE RIGHTS ARE THE SAME:
• Right to remain silent
• Right to an attorney
• Parents/guardians must be notified

DO NOT LET MINORS TALK WITHOUT AN ATTORNEY:
Police can question minors without parents in most states after Miranda is read. A minor''s statements are fully admissible.

TRANSFER TO ADULT COURT: For serious offenses, prosecutors can request transfer — resulting in adult prosecution and sentencing.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (15,'Probation and Parole: The Rules','Probation/Parole','PROBATION: Supervised release INSTEAD of prison.
PAROLE: Early supervised release FROM prison.

COMMON CONDITIONS:
• Regular reporting to probation officer
• No new arrests
• No leaving jurisdiction without permission
• Drug testing
• No contact with victims
• Employment or school
• No weapons
• Pay fines and restitution

VIOLATION CONSEQUENCES:
• Warning → Additional conditions → Short jail → REVOCATION (original sentence)

IF ARRESTED ON PROBATION/PAROLE:
• You have reduced due process rights
• Your PO will be notified immediately
• Revocation hearing can begin before new case resolves
• Get an attorney IMMEDIATELY — you face two simultaneous proceedings',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (16,'Federal vs. State Charges','Federal Cases','FEDERAL IS MORE SERIOUS:
• No parole (abolished 1987)
• Mandatory minimums for drugs and weapons
• Must serve at least 85% of sentence
• Conviction rate 90%+

FEDERAL CHARGES APPLY WHEN:
• Crimes cross state lines
• Drug trafficking (over threshold amounts or via mail)
• Federal firearms violations
• Tax crimes, wire fraud, mail fraud
• RICO / organized crime
• Immigration crimes

IF FBI CONTACTS YOU:
• Do NOT speak without an attorney — even to "clear things up"
• Federal agents are trained interrogators
• Your "innocent explanation" may be used as evidence of obstruction
• Federal investigations often precede arrests by months',25,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (17,'Plea Deals: What You Need to Know','Court Process','90-95% of convictions come from plea deals, not trials.

TYPES:
• Charge reduction: Plead to lesser charge (felony → misdemeanor)
• Sentence recommendation: Prosecutor suggests specific sentence
• Count dismissal: Some charges dropped
• Deferred prosecution: Charges dismissed after completing conditions
• Alford plea: Plead guilty while maintaining innocence

BEFORE ACCEPTING:
• Understand EVERY consequence (immigration, housing, jobs, guns)
• Know what you give up (right to trial, most appeal rights)
• Ask about diversion programs
• Your attorney MUST advise on immigration consequences (Padilla v. Kentucky)

QUESTIONS FOR YOUR ATTORNEY:
1. Likely outcome at trial vs. plea?
2. Collateral consequences of this conviction?
3. Am I eligible for diversion?
4. Can I appeal if I take this plea?',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (18,'Immigration Consequences of Criminal Charges','Immigration','A criminal conviction can result in deportation even for lawful permanent residents.

DEPORTABLE OFFENSES:
• Any "aggravated felony" (broad — includes many misdemeanors)
• Drug offenses (even simple possession)
• Domestic violence and stalking
• "Crimes of moral turpitude"
• Firearms offenses

EVEN A PLEA CAN AFFECT IMMIGRATION:
Padilla v. Kentucky (2010) — your criminal attorney MUST advise on immigration consequences before any plea.

IF YOU ARE NOT A US CITIZEN:
• Tell your attorney before any plea
• Get immigration attorney consultation on any criminal matter
• Some charges can be structured to avoid deportation consequences

IF ICE CONTACTS YOU:
• Right to remain silent
• Right to speak to an attorney
• Do not let ICE into your home without a warrant signed by a judge',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (19,'Weapons Charges: What You Need to Know','Weapons','FEDERAL LAW PROHIBITS POSSESSION BY:
• Convicted felons
• Domestic violence misdemeanants
• Those under restraining orders
• Drug users or addicts
• Those adjudicated mentally ill
• Undocumented immigrants

COMMON STATE CHARGES:
• Carrying without permit
• Carrying in prohibited place (courthouse, school, bar)
• Felon in possession (10+ years federal)
• Brandishing/menacing
• Illegal modification (suppressors, auto conversion)

IF CHARGED:
• 4th Amendment issues are common — was the search lawful?
• Prior record dramatically affects sentencing
• Federal felon-in-possession has mandatory minimums

CONCEALED CARRY: Your permit from home state does NOT automatically work in other states. Check every state before crossing.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (20,'Grand Jury: What Happens There?','Court Process','A grand jury (16-23 citizens) hears prosecutor evidence and decides whether to issue an indictment.

KEY FACTS:
• Only in federal court and about half of state courts
• Meets in SECRET — no judge, no defense attorney, no defendant
• Standard: probable cause (much lower than "beyond reasonable doubt")
• Grand juries indict ~99% of the time
• Required for all federal felony charges

IF YOU RECEIVE A GRAND JURY SUBPOENA:
• Get an attorney BEFORE doing anything else
• You can invoke the 5th Amendment
• Your attorney cannot enter the room with you — but you can step out to consult
• Witnesses frequently become targets',15,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (21,'California: Know Your Rights','State Rights - CA','California has some of the strongest defendant rights in the US.

STOP AND IDENTIFY: California does NOT have a stop-and-identify law. You are not required to give your name when stopped, only when driving.

DRUG POSSESSION: Prop 47 reclassified most drug possession to misdemeanor. Prop 64 legalized recreational cannabis. Most simple possession is cite-and-release.

SEARCH AND SEIZURE: California has broader privacy protections than federal law under Article 1, Section 13 of the CA Constitution.

FIREARMS: Requires Firearm Safety Certificate, 10-day waiting period, bans assault weapons and magazines over 10 rounds.

EXPUNGEMENT: Penal Code 1203.4 allows expungement for probation cases. Prop 47 and Prop 64 allow reclassification of old convictions.

CA Public Defender Association: (916) 446-2064',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (22,'Texas: Know Your Rights','State Rights - TX','Texas criminal law specifics:

STOP AND IDENTIFY: Texas CCP Art. 38.02 requires you to provide your name if lawfully arrested or detained. You do not have to show ID unless driving.

DRUG LAWS: Texas has some of the harshest drug laws in the US. Even small amounts of many substances are felonies. Cannabis is still fully illegal.

FIREARMS: Constitutional Carry state since 2021. No permit required, but felons, domestic violence convicts, and those under protective orders remain prohibited.

DUI/DWI: Blood draw warrants are common. Refusing a breathalyzer triggers automatic 180-day license suspension on first offense.

EXPUNGEMENT: Texas allows expunction for charges not resulting in conviction. Non-disclosure available for some first-offense deferred adjudications.

Texas Fair Defense Project: (512) 637-5220',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (23,'Florida: Know Your Rights','State Rights - FL','Florida criminal law specifics:

STOP AND IDENTIFY: Florida Stat. 856.021 requires you to identify yourself and explain your actions when an officer has reasonable suspicion.

DRUG LAWS: Mandatory minimum sentences for trafficking. Drug courts available for eligible defendants. Possession under 20g of cannabis is a misdemeanor.

DUI: The 10-day rule applies. After a DUI arrest, you have 10 days to request a formal review hearing with DHSMV or your license is automatically suspended.

FIREARMS: Constitutional Carry passed 2023. No permit required for open or concealed carry.

SPEEDY TRIAL RIGHTS: 175 days for felonies, 90 days for misdemeanors.

Florida Public Defender Association: (850) 488-2810',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (24,'New York: Know Your Rights','State Rights - NY','New York criminal law specifics:

STOP AND IDENTIFY: New York does NOT require you to provide your name during a street stop. NYPD stop-and-frisk reforms from Floyd v. City of NY significantly limit police authority.

DRUG LAWS: Possession under 3oz of marijuana decriminalized. The Marijuana Regulation and Taxation Act legalized recreational cannabis. Drug trafficking still carries serious penalties.

PRETRIAL DETENTION: 2020 bail reform eliminated cash bail for most misdemeanors and non-violent felonies.

SPEEDY TRIAL: Felonies within 6 months, misdemeanors within 90 days from arraignment.

CLEAN SLATE ACT 2023: Automatically seals most convictions. Misdemeanors after 3 years, felonies after 8 years post-sentence completion.

Legal Aid Society NYC: (212) 577-3300',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (25,'Tennessee: Know Your Rights','State Rights - TN','Tennessee criminal law specifics:

STOP AND IDENTIFY: TCA 40-7-103 requires you to provide your name, address, and explanation of your actions when lawfully detained. Refusal is a Class C misdemeanor.

DRUG LAWS: Simple possession of Schedule I-IV is a Class A misdemeanor on first offense. Schedule VI marijuana under 0.5oz is a Class A misdemeanor. Trafficking carries mandatory minimums.

DUI: Mandatory 48-hour minimum jail for first DUI. BAC over 0.20 requires mandatory 7-day minimum.

DOMESTIC VIOLENCE: Mandatory arrest policy when probable cause exists. DV conviction results in lifetime federal firearms prohibition.

EXPUNGEMENT: TCA 40-32-101 allows expungement of eligible convictions. Filing fee is $450, waivable at clinics. 5-year wait after sentence completion for felonies.

TN District Public Defenders Conference: (615) 741-1175',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (26,'Veterans Courts: What They Are and How to Get In','Veterans / Military','Veterans Treatment Courts (VTCs) are specialized courts for veterans charged with criminal offenses. There are 600 nationwide.

WHO QUALIFIES:
Must be a veteran of any branch and any era. Must have a service-connected mental health condition or substance use disorder. Criminal charge must be eligible by jurisdiction.

WHAT VTCs OFFER:
Treatment instead of incarceration. VA benefits coordination. Veteran mentors. Peer support. Dismissal or reduced charges upon successful completion.

HOW TO GET IN:
1. Tell your attorney you are a veteran immediately.
2. Obtain your DD-214 from the VA or National Personnel Records Center.
3. Request a VTC evaluation at arraignment.
4. Complete a VA mental health assessment.

Find your local VTC at justiceforvets.org or call your local VA Medical Center social work department.

Note: Dishonorable discharge may disqualify you. Consult a Veterans Service Organization first.',25,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (27,'UCMJ: Military Justice Is Different','Veterans / Military','Active duty military operates under two legal systems: civilian courts and the Uniform Code of Military Justice.

KEY DIFFERENCES:
Military can prosecute for conduct that is not a civilian crime such as adultery, AWOL, missing movement, and disrespect. Court-martial conviction can result in dishonorable discharge and loss of all VA benefits. Your civilian attorney cannot represent you at court-martial unless also a JAG officer. You have the right to free military defense counsel.

TYPES OF COURT-MARTIAL:
Summary: Minor offenses, 30-day max confinement, no jury.
Special: Mid-level, 1-year max, 3-member panel.
General: Most serious, unlimited punishment.

ARTICLE 31 RIGHTS: The military equivalent of Miranda with broader protections.

IF ARRESTED BY CIVILIAN POLICE: You face both civilian prosecution and potential military action. Notify your command immediately and request both JAG and civilian counsel.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (28,'Mental Health Holds: 5150, Baker Act, and State Laws','Mental Health','INVOLUNTARY PSYCHIATRIC HOLD LAWS BY STATE:

California 5150 WIC: 72-hour hold for danger to self or others, or gravely disabled. Police, doctors, or mental health professionals can initiate.

Florida Baker Act: 72-hour examination hold initiated by police or mental health professionals.

Texas 3206: Emergency detention up to 48 hours.

New York 9.41 MHL: Up to 72 hours.

Most other states have similar 72-hour emergency holds.

IF YOU ARE ON A MENTAL HEALTH HOLD:
This is not a criminal arrest. No Miranda rights are required. You cannot be held beyond the statutory period without a court order. You have the right to contact an attorney. A hold cannot be used directly as evidence of a crime.

IF A HOLD LEADS TO A CRIMINAL CHARGE:
Competency evaluations may be ordered. Insanity defense may be raised. Mental health diversion programs may be available. Mental illness history can affect bail conditions.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (29,'Mental Health Diversion: Treatment Instead of Prison','Mental Health','Mental health diversion programs allow criminal charges to be diverted to treatment.

COMMON STATE PROGRAMS:
California PC 1001.36: Mental health diversion for any criminal charge if defendant has qualifying diagnosis.
Florida: Mental health courts in 60+ counties.
Texas MHDD: Mental Health Diversion Districts statewide.
New York CPL 730: Competency restoration programs.

TYPICAL ELIGIBILITY:
Diagnosed mental health condition per DSM-5. Condition linked to the offense. Not charged with murder, rape, or child molestation in most programs. Must agree to treatment plan.

OUTCOME IF SUCCESSFUL:
Criminal charges dismissed. No conviction on record. Treatment replaces incarceration.

HOW TO REQUEST:
1. Tell your attorney at arraignment that you have a mental health history.
2. Obtain documentation from treating providers.
3. Request a competency evaluation or diversion hearing.',25,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (30,'How to Prepare for Your Bail Hearing','Bail','A bail hearing is your first real opportunity to get out of jail. What you present matters enormously.

FACTORS JUDGES WEIGH:
Severity of the charge and evidence. Prior criminal history. Community ties including residence, family, employment, and length in the area. Flight risk including passports and ties abroad. Prior failures to appear. Danger to the community.

WHAT HELPS:
Employment letter or pay stubs showing stable income. Lease or mortgage showing stable housing. Family members present in court. Letter from employer confirming your job is waiting. Completed substance abuse program or willingness to enter one. Voluntary passport surrender. Offering electronic monitoring as a condition.

WHAT HURTS:
Outstanding warrants in other jurisdictions. Prior failures to appear. Immigration detainer holds. No stable address or employment.

WHAT TO WEAR: Business casual minimum. No orange. Clean and conservative.

WHAT YOUR ATTORNEY SHOULD ARGUE: Community ties, lack of flight risk, and willingness to comply with any court conditions.',25,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (31,'Restraining Orders: Getting One and Fighting One','Restraining Orders','TYPES OF RESTRAINING ORDERS:
Emergency Protective Order (EPO): Issued by police at the scene, typically 72 hours.
Temporary Restraining Order (TRO): 14 to 21 days, granted without a hearing.
Permanent Protective Order: After a full hearing, 1 to 5 years depending on state.
Criminal Protective Order: Issued as part of criminal proceedings.

IF AN ORDER IS ISSUED AGAINST YOU:
Comply immediately and completely. Any contact is a new crime. Do NOT contact the petitioner even if they reach out to you first. Do not go to restricted locations. Request a hearing to contest it within the time shown on the order.

FEDERAL CONSEQUENCE: A domestic violence restraining order triggers federal firearms prohibition under 18 USC 922(g)(8). You must surrender all firearms immediately.

IF YOU NEED ONE:
File at your county courthouse for free. Request an emergency protective order from police at the scene. Contact your local DV advocate for help with paperwork.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (32,'How to Read Your Charging Document','Court Process','Your charging document is the formal legal document describing what you are accused of. Understanding it is critical.

ANATOMY OF A CHARGING DOCUMENT:
1. Case number: Used for everything.
2. Court name and jurisdiction.
3. Your identifying information.
4. Counts: Each separate charge is one count.
5. Statute: The specific law allegedly violated.
6. Elements: What the prosecutor must prove for each count.
7. Date, time, and location of the alleged offense.
8. Brief description of what allegedly happened.

KEY THINGS TO FIND:
The statute number so you can look up the exact elements. The date of the offense for statute of limitations purposes. Whether the charge is a felony or misdemeanor. The number of counts, since each can result in a separate conviction.

FINE PRINT:
Charging documents can be amended as the case develops. A complaint is initial. An information or indictment is formal. You cannot be convicted of something that was not charged.',15,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (33,'Sex Offender Registry: What Registration Means','Sex Offenses','Sex offender registration is a separate consequence from your criminal sentence.

TIERS:
Tier 1 lowest: 15 to 25 year registration with less frequent reporting.
Tier 2 moderate: 25 years with quarterly reporting.
Tier 3 highest: Lifetime registration, may include community notification.

FEDERAL LAW (SORNA): The Sex Offender Registration and Notification Act requires registration in every state for qualifying offenses. Failure to register is a federal crime.

REGISTRATION REQUIREMENTS:
Regular in-person check-ins. Disclosure of address, employer, vehicle, and internet identifiers. Restrictions on where you can live and work. Cannot reside near schools, parks, or playgrounds with distances varying by state.

INTERNATIONAL TRAVEL:
Must notify authorities before international travel. Many countries deny entry to registered sex offenders.

IMMIGRATION: Sex offense conviction triggers deportation for non-citizens in virtually all cases.

BEFORE PLEADING TO ANY SEX OFFENSE: Understand exactly what tier of registration is triggered. Get an attorney who specializes in sex crimes defense.',25,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (34,'Civil Asset Forfeiture: When Police Take Your Property','Legal Rights','Civil asset forfeiture allows police to seize property suspected of being connected to a crime without charging or convicting you.

HOW IT WORKS:
Police seize your cash, car, or property. They file a civil case against the property itself, not you. Cases are literally named State v. Four Thousand Dollars in Cash. You must prove the property is innocent since the burden is reversed. If you do not challenge within the deadline, often 30 days, the government keeps it automatically.

STATES WITH STRONGEST PROTECTIONS:
New Mexico, Nebraska, California, Colorado, and Michigan require a criminal conviction before forfeiture.

IF YOUR PROPERTY IS SEIZED:
Request a receipt for everything taken at the time of seizure. Note badge numbers and agency. File a claim within the deadline shown on your notice. Contact a civil liberties attorney. The Institute for Justice has forfeiture resources at ij.org.

DO NOT WAIT. Missing the deadline waives your right to challenge the forfeiture entirely.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (35,'Victim Rights in the Criminal Justice System','Victim Rights','If you are the victim of a crime, you have legally protected rights throughout the criminal case.

CONSTITUTIONAL PROTECTIONS:
33 states have passed Marsy''s Law or an equivalent victim rights amendment. The federal Crime Victims'' Rights Act is codified at 18 USC 3771.

CORE RIGHTS:
Right to be notified of all court proceedings. Right to be present at public proceedings. Right to be heard at plea hearings and sentencing. Right to protection from the defendant. Right to restitution from the defendant. Right to information about your case and the defendant''s custody status.

VICTIM COMPENSATION:
Every state maintains a victim compensation fund. Covers medical costs, lost wages, counseling, and funeral expenses. You do not need a conviction to file a claim. File quickly as deadlines are often 1 to 2 years from the crime. Average awards range from $1,000 to $25,000.

Find your state''s program through the National Association of Crime Victim Compensation Boards at nacvcb.org.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (36,'Diversion Programs: Avoid a Conviction Entirely','Court Process','Diversion programs allow eligible defendants to complete requirements in exchange for having charges dismissed with no conviction.

COMMON TYPES:

Pre-Trial Diversion: Available before charges are filed or at arraignment. Typically for first-time, non-violent offenders. Complete community service, treatment, classes, or restitution. Case dismissed on completion.

Deferred Adjudication: You plead guilty but sentencing is deferred. Complete probation terms and the plea is withdrawn and case dismissed.

Drug Court: Intensive 12 to 24 month program with regular appearances, frequent testing, and treatment. Successful completion results in dismissal.

STATE-SPECIFIC EXAMPLES:
Tennessee: Pre-trial diversion under TCA 40-15-105.
Texas: Deferred adjudication under CCP Art. 42A.101.
Florida: Pretrial intervention programs under Fla. Stat. 948.08.
California: PC 1000 drug diversion and PC 1001.95 discretionary diversion.

ASK YOUR ATTORNEY whether you are eligible for a diversion program at your very first meeting. Once you go to trial or take a plea without asking, diversion is usually no longer available.',25,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (37,'Criminal Charges and Your Professional License','Collateral Consequences','A criminal conviction can threaten professional licenses even when you avoid prison entirely.

LICENSES MOST AFFECTED:
Medical licenses including doctors, nurses, and pharmacists. Legal licenses. Financial licenses including CPAs, securities, and insurance. Real estate broker licenses. Teaching certificates. Commercial Driver''s Licenses. Contractor licenses.

HOW IT WORKS:
Licensing boards conduct their own investigation separate from the courts. You must report charges or convictions to most licensing boards. Failure to report is itself grounds for license revocation. Boards can suspend or revoke even for arrests without conviction.

CDL SPECIFIC:
Any DUI including in a personal vehicle triggers mandatory 1-year CDL disqualification. DUI in a commercial vehicle means 1-year minimum, 3 years if carrying hazardous materials. A second DUI means lifetime CDL disqualification in most states.

BEFORE ACCEPTING ANY PLEA:
Tell your attorney what licenses you hold. Have a licensing attorney review the plea terms. Some charges can be structured to avoid mandatory reporting triggers.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (38,'Can a Landlord Evict You for an Arrest?','Housing Rights','An arrest is not a conviction and generally cannot be used as grounds for eviction on its own.

WHAT LANDLORDS CAN DO:
Run background checks that show arrests in most states. Deny rental applications based on criminal history with certain limits. Evict for lease violations related to criminal activity on the premises. Evict if conviction results in sex offender registration in many leases.

FAIR CHANCE HOUSING LAWS:
California, Washington, Colorado, and Minnesota ban blanket criminal history bans. Many cities including New York City, Seattle, San Francisco, and Denver have additional protections. HUD guidelines discourage blanket bans on people with records.

PUBLIC HOUSING:
Mandatory bans apply to methamphetamine manufacture on the premises and registered sex offenders. Discretionary bans may apply for drug crimes or crimes affecting health and safety. One-strike policies have been significantly limited by HUD guidance.

IF FACING EVICTION:
Contact legal aid immediately since eviction timelines are fast. An arrest alone does not automatically violate your lease. A conviction for activity not occurring on the property has limited lease breach authority.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (39,'Record Sealing vs. Expungement: The Real Difference','Expungement','These two terms are often used interchangeably but they are legally distinct.

EXPUNGEMENT:
The record is physically destroyed or returned to you. It is treated as if the arrest or conviction never happened. You can legally answer no on most applications asking about criminal history. Even government agencies lose access in most cases.

SEALING:
The record still exists but is hidden from most public view. Law enforcement and courts can still access it. Some government agencies and licensing boards may still see it. You may legally deny the record exists for most civilian purposes.

WHICH IS BETTER: Expungement, when available. Sealing is more common for convictions while expungement is more common for arrests without conviction.

AUTOMATIC SEALING STATES AS OF 2024:
California under PC 1203.4. Michigan after 7 years for qualifying offenses. New York under the Clean Slate Act after waiting periods. Pennsylvania for non-conviction records. Utah for certain offenses. Washington via motion to vacate.

BOTH HELP WITH: Employment background checks, housing applications, and in some states restoration of voting rights.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (40,'When Federal vs. State Courts Have Jurisdiction','Federal Cases','The same criminal act can sometimes be charged in both federal and state court. This is not double jeopardy.

FEDERAL JURISDICTION TRIGGERS:
Crime occurred on federal property such as a national park, military base, or post office. Crime crossed state lines including kidnapping, trafficking, and wire or mail fraud. Crime involved federal employees or agencies. Immigration crimes. Crimes involving firearms during drug trafficking. Specific federal statutes including RICO and federal drug conspiracy.

STATE JURISDICTION:
Most common crimes including DUI, simple assault, theft, and state drug charges. Crimes occurring entirely within one state.

WHEN BOTH CAN CHARGE YOU:
Drug trafficking crossing state lines. Bank robbery since federal banks are federally insured. Murder of a federal officer. Civil rights violations.

IF FACING FEDERAL CHARGES:
Federal investigations can last years before charges are filed. Federal prosecutors typically only bring cases they expect to win. The federal conviction rate exceeds 90 percent. Federal public defenders exist but private counsel is often preferred for serious charges.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (41,'No-Contact Orders: What You Cannot Do','Court Process','A no-contact order issued as a condition of your release is different from a civil restraining order.

WHAT A NO-CONTACT ORDER PROHIBITS:
Direct contact including calls, texts, emails, and messages. Indirect contact meaning having someone else deliver messages for you. Being within a certain distance of the protected person, usually 100 to 500 feet. Going to their home, workplace, school, or regular locations.

COMMON MISTAKE: The protected person contacts you and you respond. This violates the order. The order applies to you, not to them. Their initiation does not give you permission to respond.

CONSEQUENCES OF VIOLATION:
New criminal charge, often a felony. Immediate bail revocation and return to custody. Significant damage to your underlying case. May trigger the federal domestic violence firearms prohibition.

HOW TO GET IT MODIFIED:
Only through a court motion. Both parties typically must agree but the judge decides. Contact your attorney. This is not something to handle yourself.

IF YOU LIVE TOGETHER: You may need to find alternative housing immediately. Family court can address housing and custody issues as separate proceedings.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (42,'Deportable Crimes: What Non-Citizens Must Know','Immigration','For non-citizens, a criminal conviction can result in permanent deportation even after serving a sentence.

CATEGORIES THAT TRIGGER REMOVAL:

Aggravated Felonies as defined under federal law include murder, rape, sexual abuse of a minor, drug trafficking, firearms trafficking, crimes of violence with a sentence of one or more years, theft with a sentence of one or more years, and fraud with losses over $10,000.

Crimes Involving Moral Turpitude include murder, rape, robbery, arson, and many fraud and deceit crimes.

Drug Offenses: Almost any drug conviction can trigger removal.

Firearms Offenses: Illegal possession and trafficking.

Domestic Violence: Conviction is automatic grounds for removal.

EVEN A MISDEMEANOR CAN DEPORT YOU: The federal definition of aggravated felony includes many state misdemeanors.

IF YOU ARE NOT A CITIZEN:
Tell your criminal attorney your immigration status before entering any plea. Request an immigration attorney consultation since your criminal attorney may not know all immigration consequences. Some charges can be pled to in ways that avoid deportation triggers, such as accepting 364 days instead of 365.',25,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (43,'How Criminal Appeals Work','Appeals','Most people convicted at trial or by plea have limited appeal rights.

APPEAL VS. NEW TRIAL MOTION:
An appeal asks a higher court to review the lower court''s legal errors. A new trial motion is filed in the same court, usually for newly discovered evidence.

GROUNDS FOR APPEAL:
Constitutional violations under the 4th, 5th, or 6th Amendment. Ineffective assistance of counsel requiring proof of both attorney error and resulting prejudice. Prosecutorial misconduct. Insufficient evidence to support the verdict. Incorrect jury instructions. Newly discovered evidence unavailable at trial.

CRITICAL TIMELINE:
Notice of appeal is due within 30 days after sentencing in most state courts and 14 days in federal court. Missing this deadline waives your right to appeal.

GUILTY PLEAS LIMIT APPEALS: Pleading guilty waives most appeal rights. Remaining grounds include constitutionality of the statute, sentence exceeding the maximum, and challenges to whether the plea was knowing and voluntary.

POST-CONVICTION RELIEF:
Habeas corpus allows federal review of state convictions claiming a federal rights violation. State Post-Conviction Relief provides a state-level remedy for constitutional violations. State Innocence Projects accept cases involving new evidence.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (44,'Traffic Stops: What You Must and Cannot Do','Arrest Rights','Traffic stops are the most common police encounter. Knowing the rules prevents escalation.

YOU MUST:
Pull over safely and promptly. Provide your driver''s license, registration, and insurance when asked. Provide your name since you are operating a vehicle.

YOU DO NOT HAVE TO:
Answer questions about where you are going or coming from. Consent to a search of your vehicle. Step out of the vehicle unless ordered to by police.

CAR SEARCHES:
Officers need probable cause, your consent, or a warrant to search. If you consent, you waive Fourth Amendment protections. Saying I do not consent to a search is your legal right. If they search anyway, do not resist. Document everything and tell your attorney.

PASSENGERS:
In stop-and-identify states, must provide ID if there is reasonable suspicion. Generally have the same right to silence as the driver.

COMMON SCENARIOS:
Smell of marijuana is probable cause in states where cannabis remains illegal and contested in legal states. An open container in plain view is probable cause for a search. An outstanding warrant on your record allows arrest regardless of what prompted the stop.

ALWAYS: Stay calm, keep hands visible, and document badge numbers when it is safe to do so.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (45,'Felony Classifications: What the Letters and Numbers Mean','Court Process','Every state organizes felony severity differently.

LETTER SYSTEM used by most states:
Class A or 1: Most serious including murder and kidnapping. Range of 10 to 99 years or life.
Class B or 2: Serious violence and major drug trafficking. Typically 2 to 20 years.
Class C or 3: Significant but less severe including robbery and aggravated assault. Typically 1 to 10 years.
Class D or 4: Mid-range including theft and drug possession. Probation up to 5 years.
Class E, F, 5, or 6: Lowest felony level, often eligible for probation or diversion.

FEDERAL SYSTEM: Uses offense levels 1 through 43 in the US Sentencing Guidelines rather than letter grades.

WHY IT MATTERS:
Determines maximum sentence. Determines eligibility for parole and probation. Determines which collateral consequences apply including housing and employment restrictions. Affects future sentencing for repeat offenses through recidivism enhancements.

WOBBLERS: Some offenses can be charged as either a felony or misdemeanor at the prosecutor''s discretion. Avoiding a felony conviction is worth almost any negotiation effort because the collateral consequences last a lifetime.',15,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (46,'DNA Evidence: How It Works in Criminal Cases','Legal Rights','DNA evidence is powerful but not infallible.

HOW DNA IS COLLECTED:
Swabs from crime scenes, victims, and suspects. Touch DNA from surfaces. Database comparison through CODIS, the Combined DNA Index System.

IF YOUR DNA IS IN CODIS:
Every state collects DNA from felony arrests and many states also collect for misdemeanors. DNA stays in CODIS even if charges are dropped. You can petition for removal in some states after dismissal.

DEFENSE CHALLENGES TO DNA:
Chain of custody issues questioning whether evidence was handled properly. Lab contamination, which is common in high-volume labs. Mixed DNA profiles where multiple contributors make interpretation subjective. Statistical probability claims where match probability is not certainty. Secondary transfer where your DNA at a scene does not mean you were physically present.

IF ACCUSED BASED ON DNA:
Request the full lab report and analyst''s notes. Hire an independent forensic expert to review the findings. DNA match statistics can range from one in ten thousand to one in a trillion, and that difference matters enormously at trial.

COLD CASES: DNA has reopened cases more than 30 years old. Genetic genealogy through ancestry databases has identified suspects from partial DNA.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (47,'Electronic Monitoring and Ankle Monitors','Bail','Electronic monitoring is increasingly used as an alternative to cash bail or detention.

TYPES:
GPS ankle bracelet tracks location 24 hours per day and reports to monitoring company and authorities. SCRAM bracelet detects alcohol through skin perspiration. Home detention monitor detects if you leave a defined radius. Phone check-in systems use random calls requiring voice confirmation.

IF ORDERED TO WEAR A MONITOR:
Do not tamper with it since tampering is a separate felony. Charge it as directed, usually one to two hours per day. Report any malfunction immediately in writing. Know your approved zones and schedule exactly.

COMMON VIOLATIONS:
Leaving an approved area even briefly. SCRAM alert for alcohol, which can be triggered by certain foods or hand sanitizer so report any anomaly immediately. Failure to charge the device. Removing or covering the device.

COSTS: Many jurisdictions charge defendants $5 to $15 per day for monitoring. If you cannot afford this, tell your attorney. Courts cannot revoke bail solely for inability to pay fees under Bearden v. Georgia, 1983.

VIOLATION CONSEQUENCE: Immediate arrest warrant and bail revocation in most jurisdictions.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (48,'Mugshots and Removing Your Record Online','Expungement','Your arrest record including your mugshot is often public record. Here is what you can do about it.

WHY MUGSHOTS APPEAR ONLINE:
Many states have open arrest records laws. Third-party websites scrape booking photos from county jail websites. These sites often charge removal fees which is legal but predatory.

FREE REMOVAL OPTIONS:
Expungement is the most effective remedy. Once expunged, many state laws require these sites to remove your information.
12 or more states now have mugshot removal laws requiring sites to comply for free upon request including CA, TX, GA, OR, WA, and IL.
After expungement, submit a legal removal request to Google to deindex the page.
Most reputable sites have a free removal process for dismissed or expunged cases.

DO NOT PAY mugshot removal services. This rarely works permanently.

EMPLOYMENT BACKGROUND CHECKS:
After expungement, submit a correction request directly to commercial services like Checkr and HireRight. Ban the Box laws in 37 states and 150 or more cities limit when employers can ask about records.

FCRA RIGHTS: You have the right to dispute inaccurate criminal history on background reports under the Fair Credit Reporting Act.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (49,'The 10 Most Common Probation Violations','Probation/Parole','Probation revocation can result in serving your full original sentence in prison.

1. FAILED DRUG TEST: Most common violation. Even marijuana in legal states can violate probation if your order prohibits it.
2. MISSING A CHECK-IN: Call your PO proactively if you cannot make it. Silence always makes it worse.
3. FAILURE TO PAY FINES: Willful non-payment can trigger a violation, but inability to pay alone cannot revoke probation under Bearden v. Georgia.
4. NEW ARREST: Even without a conviction, a new arrest can trigger a violation hearing.
5. LEAVING THE JURISDICTION: Travel almost always requires advance written permission.
6. CHANGING ADDRESS WITHOUT NOTICE: Report address changes immediately and in writing.
7. ASSOCIATING WITH FELONS: Many probation orders prohibit contact with anyone who has a felony conviction.
8. POSSESSION OF WEAPONS: Even legally owned firearms may be prohibited by your order.
9. SOCIAL MEDIA VIOLATIONS: Posts about criminal activity, drugs, or weapons can be used as evidence.
10. FAILURE TO COMPLETE REQUIRED PROGRAMS: Community service, classes, and treatment programs all have deadlines that must be met.

IF YOU VIOLATED: Disclose it proactively. Get an attorney before your next PO meeting if possible. Document all mitigating circumstances in writing. A single violation does not automatically mean revocation since judges have broad discretion.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5),
  (50,'Coming Home: Reentry Resources After Incarceration','Reentry','The first 72 hours after release are critical. Preparation dramatically reduces recidivism.

IMMEDIATE NEEDS IN THE FIRST 72 HOURS:
Housing: Contact family, a halfway house, or a reentry shelter before release if at all possible.
ID: Your Social Security card and birth certificate are typically returned at release. Get a state ID at the DMV within 24 hours.
Medication: Ensure you have a supply of any prescription medications.
Food: Apply for SNAP benefits immediately. Most drug conviction bans have been repealed.
Reporting: Know your first probation or parole check-in date exactly.

FEDERAL RESOURCES:
US Attorneys Reentry Resources at justice.gov/reentry.
CSG Justice Center at csgjusticecenter.org.
211 hotline connects you to local food, shelter, and services.

BENEFITS YOU MAY QUALIFY FOR:
SNAP food stamps in most states. Medicaid, which covers many people with felony records following ACA expansion. Social Security if you had a qualifying disability. Veterans benefits regardless of incarceration unless you received a dishonorable discharge.

RIGHTS RESTORATION:
Voting: Most states restore voting rights upon release or after completing probation. Firearms: Federal felony conviction means a lifetime ban. Some states allow restoration after a waiting period. Public housing: Bans are narrowing in many jurisdictions. Contact your local housing authority.',20,NULL,NULL,'beginner',5)
